WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.981 --> 00:00:04.821
Laws have been translated into
software by hand for decades.

2
00:00:05.963 --> 00:00:10.963
Every implementing organisation has its
own interpretation of the law, and a large

3
00:00:10.963 --> 00:00:13.963
part of that interpretation
ends up in software.

4
00:00:13.955 --> 00:00:19.355
That has gone on for years, decades in some
cases, and countless amendments and new

5
00:00:19.355 --> 00:00:22.675
bills end up stacked on top
of each other in software.

6
00:00:24.826 --> 00:00:27.327
As a result, there is
no overview any more.

7
00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:31.887
It's unclear which piece of software relates
to which piece of legislation, and the

8
00:00:31.887 --> 00:00:33.450
other way round.

9
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.951
Which in turn makes it unpredictable
how that software works.

10
00:00:37.951 --> 00:00:42.511
Not only for citizens and businesses on the
receiving end of that software, but also

11
00:00:42.511 --> 00:00:47.391
for policymakers, for civil servants who
work with it, and even for IT people, the

12
00:00:47.391 --> 00:00:48.671
ones who build it.

13
00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:59.354
It becomes unmanageable to change that
software, people no longer dare, and no

14
00:00:59.354 --> 00:01:01.594
longer know the effect of a change.

15
00:01:06.497 --> 00:01:11.057
RegelRecht asks: what if we start
with the law, make the law itself

16
00:01:11.057 --> 00:01:14.676
machine-executable, and
publish that openly?

17
00:01:14.676 --> 00:01:18.956
I'm Anne Schuth, staff engineer at RegelRecht,
and I'll be giving the demo for about

18
00:01:18.956 --> 00:01:20.596
the next quarter of an hour.

19
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.180
This isn't static.

20
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.460
You can stop at any moment
and click around yourself.

21
00:01:30.462 --> 00:01:34.544
On the left, questions
I get very often appear.

22
00:01:34.544 --> 00:01:37.059
You can click them to get answers.

23
00:01:37.059 --> 00:01:38.560
Done.

24
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.840
It's working software, but
not a finished product.

25
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.468
So it's not meant to
ever go into production.

26
00:01:45.392 --> 00:01:49.791
And we do this from a policy
department, so we make policy by doing.

27
00:01:49.715 --> 00:01:54.155
We start at the very back end, with a
rulework, in this case for the healthcare

28
00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:54.995
allowance.

29
00:01:55.839 --> 00:02:00.022
Here we see a machine-executable
version of the healthcare allowance.

30
00:02:00.022 --> 00:02:03.864
Not the whole law, only article 2 and 4.

31
00:02:03.864 --> 00:02:08.544
Here's article 2, the whole article,
so here we see the full text.

32
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:13.395
And below it we see machine-readable,
the machine-executable part of that law.

33
00:02:13.395 --> 00:02:15.016
Dat is onze interpretatie.

34
00:02:15.016 --> 00:02:19.552
And what we see here is that it
starts with a parameter, namely BSN.

35
00:02:19.478 --> 00:02:22.860
Well, someone must be
old enough and insured.

36
00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.940
Well, someone must at least be
of minimum age and be insured.

37
00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:30.560
And then we see the amount
of healthcare allowance.

38
00:02:30.485 --> 00:02:36.565
That's a whole calculation I won't go into,
but below we see Operation Add: adding

39
00:02:36.565 --> 00:02:41.016
income to partner income, to compare
it with the threshold income.

40
00:02:41.016 --> 00:02:44.536
What I skipped over just now
is something we call inputs.

41
00:02:45.941 --> 00:02:48.661
Those are really references to other laws.

42
00:02:49.465 --> 00:02:53.349
For example, 'is insured',
one of the conditions.

43
00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:54.309
From another law.

44
00:02:54.309 --> 00:02:58.213
So the act points to insured,
per the health insurance act.

45
00:02:58.133 --> 00:03:01.859
So we can click through to the health
insurance act, and we'll do that now, and then

46
00:03:01.859 --> 00:03:03.824
we see what it means to be insured.

47
00:03:03.756 --> 00:03:08.476
Someone is insured, that's an output
here, if they have insurance or treaty

48
00:03:08.476 --> 00:03:11.436
insurance and if they're not in detention.

49
00:03:13.221 --> 00:03:17.494
And being in detention is again an input,
as we see here, from the penitentiary

50
00:03:17.494 --> 00:03:18.607
principles act.

51
00:03:18.535 --> 00:03:22.737
And we can go there too, and then we
see what it means to be detained.

52
00:03:22.737 --> 00:03:27.657
Namely, you're in a penitentiary or a
remand centre, and you're confined or

53
00:03:27.657 --> 00:03:29.881
temporarily absent.

54
00:03:29.881 --> 00:03:33.801
And so you see that to calculate healthcare
allowance, the entitlement and the

55
00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:37.001
amount, you need a whole
series of legislation.

56
00:03:40.047 --> 00:03:42.308
And actually, it's a graph.

57
00:03:42.308 --> 00:03:46.408
And once you have laws machine-executable,
you can also visualise that.

58
00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:49.576
Then you can see that those laws
are connected to each other.

59
00:03:49.576 --> 00:03:54.136
I'll zoom in a bit, but here you see,
for example, the healthcare allowance.

60
00:03:57.282 --> 00:04:01.922
Let's see, here we see 'is
insured', we can follow that line.

61
00:04:03.568 --> 00:04:07.842
Then we reach the Health Insurance Act and
see 'is detained', and follow that line

62
00:04:07.842 --> 00:04:08.483
too.

63
00:04:08.412 --> 00:04:09.772
We see that here.

64
00:04:10.655 --> 00:04:14.095
Then we see the determination
of the detention status.

65
00:04:14.906 --> 00:04:19.186
And here we actually see the whole system
of legislation needed to arrive at the

66
00:04:19.186 --> 00:04:20.691
healthcare allowance.

67
00:04:20.691 --> 00:04:24.611
If I click this, we see all the legislation
we currently have in the proof of

68
00:04:24.611 --> 00:04:25.651
concept.

69
00:04:26.776 --> 00:04:28.456
That's quite a queue.

70
00:04:29.858 --> 00:04:32.018
Have a guess which one this might be.

71
00:04:33.847 --> 00:04:35.847
Feel free to look around yourself.

72
00:04:37.671 --> 00:04:42.631
You can click around, zoom in, zoom out
and follow the lines to see how law and

73
00:04:42.631 --> 00:04:44.151
regulation connect.

74
00:04:47.983 --> 00:04:49.594
We'll pick up shortly.

75
00:04:49.594 --> 00:04:53.514
One question I get a lot here
is how we deal with ambiguity.

76
00:04:59.941 --> 00:05:05.021
So if two definitions with the same name
appear somewhere in this network, partner

77
00:05:05.021 --> 00:05:07.461
income is a common one, or household.

78
00:05:14.288 --> 00:05:17.168
And they do occur, you
could go and look for them.

79
00:05:19.115 --> 00:05:25.115
So in different places, in different laws,
partner income or household is defined in

80
00:05:25.115 --> 00:05:26.235
different ways.

81
00:05:28.163 --> 00:05:31.303
And we humans find that really annoying.

82
00:05:31.303 --> 00:05:35.367
But for machines it doesn't matter, as
long as you keep pointing to the right

83
00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:36.070
definition.

84
00:05:35.992 --> 00:05:40.552
What you see here can be generated
automatically once you have machine-executable

85
00:05:40.552 --> 00:05:42.232
law and regulation.

86
00:05:45.561 --> 00:05:50.361
And this is already quite useful for legal
analysts, legislative lawyers, anyone

87
00:05:50.361 --> 00:05:54.441
working with law and regulation, because
it shows what is all connected and what

88
00:05:54.441 --> 00:05:56.681
might break if you change something.

89
00:06:04.761 --> 00:06:09.403
Once your law is machine-executable,
you can also do the following.

90
00:06:09.403 --> 00:06:13.083
What we're looking at here
are scenarios, cases.

91
00:06:14.244 --> 00:06:19.284
And those cases are very often described in
the explanatory memorandum, for example.

92
00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:27.810
So a law goes to Parliament, or a bill, or
an amendment, and usually an explanatory

93
00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.810
memorandum is sent along with it.

94
00:06:31.156 --> 00:06:36.519
And that explanatory memorandum describes
how the law should work in specific cases.

95
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:41.720
Sometimes even a whole table with properties
of people or households, businesses,

96
00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:43.880
showing how the law should apply to them.

97
00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:48.690
The intended way the law would work.

98
00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:52.290
So you can take such a case from
its explanatory memorandum.

99
00:06:53.476 --> 00:06:54.820
And it looks like this.

100
00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:59.618
So we see a person with a
fictional BSN, fictional wage.

101
00:06:59.618 --> 00:07:03.658
And when the healthcare allowance act is
executed, this should be the healthcare

102
00:07:03.658 --> 00:07:07.881
allowance in euro cents per
year for this specific case.

103
00:07:07.881 --> 00:07:11.608
Now, what we can do, and this is why
it's called machine-executable law and

104
00:07:11.608 --> 00:07:13.472
regulation, is run it.

105
00:07:13.403 --> 00:07:18.443
And what we get is what we call a trace of
the law being executed on this specific

106
00:07:18.443 --> 00:07:19.003
case.

107
00:07:20.205 --> 00:07:23.746
Here, for example, we
see the law executing.

108
00:07:23.746 --> 00:07:27.426
To determine the healthcare
allowance for the BSN we just saw.

109
00:07:28.731 --> 00:07:33.171
And a bit further down, we see the Health
Insurance Act was executed to determine

110
00:07:33.171 --> 00:07:35.518
whether someone is insured.

111
00:07:35.518 --> 00:07:39.607
And further down still, we recognise
the Penitentiary Principles Act, used to

112
00:07:39.607 --> 00:07:41.651
determine whether someone is detained.

113
00:07:41.583 --> 00:07:45.227
In this case, this person is not detained.

114
00:07:45.227 --> 00:07:47.600
So it simply carries on.

115
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.160
And then all the way down, quite a scroll,
we see the amount of healthcare allowance

116
00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.400
in euro cents per year, which here
matches what we saw in this case.

117
00:08:04.805 --> 00:08:07.950
Under 18, no healthcare allowance.

118
00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:10.273
So this is another scenario.

119
00:08:10.273 --> 00:08:14.033
And when we run it, we see the
trace has become much shorter.

120
00:08:15.081 --> 00:08:20.668
For example, we see this person is 17,
and that stops it from going any further.

121
00:08:20.668 --> 00:08:22.076
That was one condition.

122
00:08:22.012 --> 00:08:25.794
Someone had to be at least
the minimum age, which was 18.

123
00:08:25.794 --> 00:08:27.074
So it stops there.

124
00:08:27.074 --> 00:08:31.234
So this person does not meet the conditions,
so the amount of healthcare allowance

125
00:08:31.234 --> 00:08:36.247
is not determined, and sensitive data points,
such as whether they are in detention,

126
00:08:36.247 --> 00:08:37.794
are not even looked at.

127
00:08:42.741 --> 00:08:46.981
So there is a kind of data
minimisation built in automatically.

128
00:08:47.876 --> 00:08:50.276
Feel free to have a look around here too.

129
00:08:53.158 --> 00:08:56.918
You can play with those scenarios
yourself and follow the traces.

130
00:08:58.968 --> 00:09:04.008
One of the questions I often get, or have
often had, is how we actually arrive at

131
00:09:04.008 --> 00:09:05.528
those ruleworks.

132
00:09:09.426 --> 00:09:14.506
The short answer is that we use generative
AI to turn legislation and regulations

133
00:09:14.506 --> 00:09:16.386
into a rulework like that.

134
00:09:18.825 --> 00:09:23.785
We use generative AI to turn the
explanatory memorandum into a scenario.

135
00:09:25.454 --> 00:09:29.134
Then we run the scenario and
find out it doesn't work yet.

136
00:09:30.235 --> 00:09:33.056
So some things did not pass.

137
00:09:33.056 --> 00:09:37.576
And we take the output, the results, the
things that go wrong, and give it to the

138
00:09:37.576 --> 00:09:39.616
language model to go another round.

139
00:09:40.539 --> 00:09:45.200
So again a rulework is pulled
out, again a scenario.

140
00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:49.881
Then it runs again until, at some
point, everything shows as passed.

141
00:09:49.881 --> 00:09:54.201
That's the point where we'd hand it to
a legal analyst or legislative lawyer.

142
00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:58.410
So that would be the starting point
for a lawyer to look at how the

143
00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:03.130
machine-executable law and regulation works,
and whether it does what it should and

144
00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:06.090
implements the law and
regulation the way we want.

145
00:10:08.438 --> 00:10:12.838
Once you make laws machine-executable,
you can do all sorts of things.

146
00:10:12.782 --> 00:10:15.662
You can also run it on
several people at once.

147
00:10:17.646 --> 00:10:22.389
So in the scenarios, we ran the
law on one individual at a time.

148
00:10:22.389 --> 00:10:26.949
And here we're running it on a
population, in this case 50 people.

149
00:10:28.654 --> 00:10:34.590
Average age 54, 36% partnered,
40% tenants, 18% with children,

150
00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:38.012
4% students, income 41,000 euros.

151
00:10:37.942 --> 00:10:43.142
It's a fairly small population, so not
very realistic, but sampled from CBS

152
00:10:43.142 --> 00:10:44.342
statistics.

153
00:10:50.812 --> 00:10:56.092
Once you have all the laws, which we see
below, so here we see social assistance,

154
00:10:56.092 --> 00:11:01.772
income-related contribution, ZVW, health
insurance act, income tax, voting

155
00:11:01.772 --> 00:11:05.212
rights, House of Representatives,
et cetera, et cetera.

156
00:11:06.188 --> 00:11:10.988
Once all those laws are machine-executable
and run on such a population, you can

157
00:11:10.988 --> 00:11:12.668
calculate things like this.

158
00:11:13.541 --> 00:11:16.885
Disposable income, so what you
keep at the end of the month.

159
00:11:16.885 --> 00:11:22.411
So income minus tax plus the allowances
and benefits the laws grant.

160
00:11:22.411 --> 00:11:26.235
That also factors in all the
interaction between laws.

161
00:11:26.235 --> 00:11:29.819
So you might get more somewhere,
and less somewhere else.

162
00:11:29.819 --> 00:11:32.856
And you see that in amounts like these.

163
00:11:32.856 --> 00:11:36.936
Once laws are executable, you can
look at the effect of amendments.

164
00:11:40.943 --> 00:11:45.423
For example, an amendment to the default
premium of the healthcare allowance.

165
00:11:46.481 --> 00:11:51.041
If we roughly double it and run a simulation
on the same population, we see what

166
00:11:51.041 --> 00:11:53.041
happens and can compare them.

167
00:11:57.610 --> 00:12:01.690
So here we see the comparison between the
default situation and the one with the

168
00:12:01.690 --> 00:12:03.530
default premium changed.

169
00:12:04.842 --> 00:12:09.442
So here, for example, disposable
income per month goes up from 4720 to

170
00:12:09.442 --> 00:12:13.722
4775 euros.

171
00:12:19.321 --> 00:12:23.241
And if you have to vote, say you're in
the House of Representatives voting on an

172
00:12:23.241 --> 00:12:28.201
amendment changing that default premium,
it's very useful to have this: the effect

173
00:12:28.201 --> 00:12:30.281
of an amendment to the
healthcare allowance.

174
00:12:33.761 --> 00:12:37.121
We see the healthcare allowance
act goes up considerably.

175
00:12:43.405 --> 00:12:47.645
Apparently people are being cut somewhere
else, and you want to know that.

176
00:12:48.766 --> 00:12:52.926
Here you can have a click around yourself,
maybe try out some other law amendments

177
00:12:52.926 --> 00:12:54.766
and make different comparisons.

178
00:12:58.433 --> 00:13:02.535
And you can zoom in on how this
breaks down within a population.

179
00:13:02.535 --> 00:13:07.855
So here you can see, for example, how it
breaks down by age, by income, by whether

180
00:13:07.855 --> 00:13:12.375
you have a partner, whether you rent
or own, and whether you have children.

181
00:13:15.378 --> 00:13:17.219
So feel free to look around.

182
00:13:17.219 --> 00:13:18.819
We'll pick up shortly.

183
00:13:20.387 --> 00:13:24.889
Once you make laws machine-executable,
you can do this too.

184
00:13:24.889 --> 00:13:28.089
What we see here is a
kind of My government.

185
00:13:31.012 --> 00:13:34.932
And what we actually see
here, let me reset the demo.

186
00:13:35.794 --> 00:13:38.674
What we see here is a portal for Merijn.

187
00:13:42.501 --> 00:13:44.598
We also have Claudia's portal.

188
00:13:44.523 --> 00:13:49.563
But Merijn, for example, may be entitled
to child budget, healthcare allowance,

189
00:13:49.563 --> 00:13:52.363
Amsterdam social assistance, and so on.

190
00:13:53.691 --> 00:13:56.414
Here we see all the laws
again that we saw earlier.

191
00:13:56.414 --> 00:14:01.374
Take that healthcare allowance: we see
exactly the trace we just saw, but only the

192
00:14:01.374 --> 00:14:03.962
data points from that trace.

193
00:14:03.962 --> 00:14:08.066
So here, for example, we see
that Merijn is not in detention.

194
00:14:08.066 --> 00:14:10.229
From the Penitentiary Principles Act.

195
00:14:10.229 --> 00:14:14.474
Merijn checks the data,
agrees, and applies.

196
00:14:14.474 --> 00:14:16.739
And it's granted straight away.

197
00:14:16.739 --> 00:14:20.979
So the Dienst Toeslagen at the back end,
the competent authority for the Healthcare

198
00:14:20.979 --> 00:14:24.505
Allowance Act, grants
this amount immediately.

199
00:14:24.505 --> 00:14:28.785
That's possible because the application
was made with machine-executable law and

200
00:14:28.785 --> 00:14:33.705
regulation for the healthcare allowance,
and Dienst Toeslagen uses exactly the same

201
00:14:33.705 --> 00:14:37.474
machine-executable law and
regulation at the back end.

202
00:14:37.474 --> 00:14:42.034
In other words, this isn't the calculator,
but the real interpretation of law and

203
00:14:42.034 --> 00:14:43.154
regulation.

204
00:14:44.062 --> 00:14:45.982
Housing benefit differs slightly.

205
00:14:45.982 --> 00:14:51.022
Merijn has to supply some data, like
the rent and eligible service costs.

206
00:14:54.349 --> 00:14:57.055
And that's 302.96 euros.

207
00:14:56.991 --> 00:15:01.154
And then Merijn perhaps thinks,
hey, I expected a bit more.

208
00:15:01.154 --> 00:15:05.747
And if he looks more closely, he also
sees that his income is completely wrong.

209
00:15:05.673 --> 00:15:08.730
He clicks again and sees
business profit listed.

210
00:15:08.655 --> 00:15:12.975
Last year Merijn may have had a
business, but I no longer have mine.

211
00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:20.161
Perhaps with a deregistration at the KVK.

212
00:15:20.161 --> 00:15:22.323
Well, Merijn submits that.

213
00:15:22.323 --> 00:15:24.051
So, Merijn submits.

214
00:15:24.051 --> 00:15:29.331
We also see the amount go
up, from 302 to 406.95

215
00:15:29.331 --> 00:15:30.451
euros.

216
00:15:30.371 --> 00:15:34.450
Now Merijn can apply for this,
and it may need to be assessed.

217
00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:39.010
As the government, we may want to look at
that change in Merijn's income, in the KVK

218
00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:40.610
deregistration proof.

219
00:15:45.112 --> 00:15:49.192
We also see that the healthcare allowance
Merijn already applied for, and was

220
00:15:49.192 --> 00:15:51.992
granted, no longer actually matches.

221
00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:59.165
That healthcare allowance was also based
on income, and Merijn has changed that.

222
00:15:59.165 --> 00:16:04.125
So Merijn may want to amend that application
too, and it may need assessing, because

223
00:16:04.125 --> 00:16:08.685
an official still has to review the
underlying supporting document, the KVK

224
00:16:08.685 --> 00:16:10.045
deregistration.

225
00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:15.350
This is a moment when people often ask
whether this couldn't have been done

226
00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:16.550
proactively.

227
00:16:19.161 --> 00:16:24.841
This demo comes from a field lab on
proactive services, in December 2024.

228
00:16:27.428 --> 00:16:30.319
And that is exactly the reason
to start working on this.

229
00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:34.450
Once we can calculate entitlement to
healthcare allowance and its amount with

230
00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:39.130
machine-executable legislation,
couldn't we also grant it proactively?

231
00:16:43.281 --> 00:16:48.481
The short answer: technically perhaps,
but it's not always desirable, because we

232
00:16:48.481 --> 00:16:50.881
don't know everything about applicants.

233
00:16:55.831 --> 00:17:00.551
The longer answer: to grant directly, we
would need to know everything about an

234
00:17:00.551 --> 00:17:01.591
applicant.

235
00:17:03.999 --> 00:17:06.581
For example, whether
someone inherited money.

236
00:17:06.581 --> 00:17:10.861
With all the data the government holds, it
may look like you qualify for healthcare

237
00:17:10.861 --> 00:17:13.861
allowance, but the government
doesn't know everything.

238
00:17:15.028 --> 00:17:18.948
If you've just received an inheritance,
say, that affects your entitlement to

239
00:17:18.948 --> 00:17:21.268
healthcare allowance and its amount.

240
00:17:25.056 --> 00:17:29.536
And if we had granted it directly, you
could end up having to repay all of it.

241
00:17:31.181 --> 00:17:33.013
So that requires choices.

242
00:17:32.943 --> 00:17:37.503
It requires political choices, and perhaps
legislative changes, for this to be done

243
00:17:37.503 --> 00:17:38.703
reliably.

244
00:17:42.237 --> 00:17:46.917
So Merijn has made these two applications,
and then there's perhaps a case system,

245
00:17:46.917 --> 00:17:50.637
here for Dienst Toeslagen, but it
could be another organisation too.

246
00:17:54.323 --> 00:17:57.363
They see Merijn's two
applications in detail.

247
00:17:58.625 --> 00:18:02.625
And then he sees, for example,
that Merijn made a change here.

248
00:18:03.587 --> 00:18:06.316
Profit fell from 25,000 to zero.

249
00:18:06.248 --> 00:18:08.209
Approve or reject.

250
00:18:08.209 --> 00:18:11.809
Or the whole application
can be granted or rejected.

251
00:18:12.711 --> 00:18:17.191
This official weighs it very carefully,
explains why this doesn't add up, and

252
00:18:17.191 --> 00:18:18.471
rejects it.

253
00:18:24.006 --> 00:18:26.607
And for Merijn, that would look like this.

254
00:18:26.607 --> 00:18:30.927
His housing benefit application
is rejected, and he can object.

255
00:18:33.609 --> 00:18:38.249
Objecting is of course another law coming
into play: the General Administrative Law

256
00:18:38.249 --> 00:18:39.451
Act.

257
00:18:39.451 --> 00:18:43.771
And that General Administrative
Law Act is also machine-executable.

258
00:18:45.353 --> 00:18:46.814
And we can see that too.

259
00:18:46.814 --> 00:18:49.374
Here we see the objection, for example.

260
00:18:51.112 --> 00:18:53.354
Then we see when you can object.

261
00:18:53.354 --> 00:18:58.114
So where in the healthcare allowance law
the amount was an output, here we see that

262
00:18:58.114 --> 00:19:00.234
objection possible is an output.

263
00:19:01.388 --> 00:19:05.823
And we see, for example, that the law the
decision came from must not be a generally

264
00:19:05.823 --> 00:19:10.257
binding regulation, not a policy rule,
not a preparatory decision of another act,

265
00:19:10.257 --> 00:19:13.763
and must result in a decision or
a decision of general application.

266
00:19:13.695 --> 00:19:16.255
And you must not have objected before.

267
00:19:16.255 --> 00:19:21.295
So you see that more procedural legislation,
like the Awb, or this part of it, I

268
00:19:21.295 --> 00:19:24.415
should say, can also be
made machine-executable.

269
00:19:27.313 --> 00:19:29.112
And that it can work along.

270
00:19:29.034 --> 00:19:34.717
So the block here, objection possible or
object, is perhaps in this user interface,

271
00:19:34.717 --> 00:19:36.220
in this piece of software.

272
00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:41.020
But the logic behind it, the business logic
that decides whether someone can object,

273
00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:43.100
sits in a rulework like this.

274
00:19:45.405 --> 00:19:49.885
And that means you only have to interpret
a piece of the Awb once, make it

275
00:19:49.885 --> 00:19:53.405
machine-executable once, to
make it work everywhere.

276
00:19:56.475 --> 00:19:59.355
The legislation we see here, for example.

277
00:20:00.858 --> 00:20:06.138
That applies not just to the Awb, but
perhaps to other procedural legislation too,

278
00:20:06.138 --> 00:20:11.338
like the Archives Act, the GDPR or
the Woo, or rather, parts of all those

279
00:20:11.338 --> 00:20:12.298
laws.

280
00:20:13.787 --> 00:20:18.827
But you can go quite far in making procedural
legislation machine-executable too.

281
00:20:21.262 --> 00:20:22.823
This is where I want to stop.

282
00:20:22.823 --> 00:20:25.703
That was RegelRecht:
law to digital effect.

283
00:20:25.703 --> 00:20:28.391
A complete story, not a finished product.

284
00:20:28.327 --> 00:20:30.996
What we do is make policy by doing.

285
00:20:30.929 --> 00:20:32.425
The demo is open.

286
00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:33.470
Try it yourself.

287
00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:36.072
Click around, or try it yourself.

288
00:20:36.072 --> 00:20:40.232
And read more about what we
do at regelrecht.rijks.app.
